block: wbt: 2 ms write-back latency target on every disk

Write-back throttling aims at 2 ms of latency on a non-rotating disk
and 75 ms on a rotating one. Use 2 ms for both.

To measure ALONE, never in the batch with the three mq-deadline and
rq_affinity experiments. It is the one of the four that can make things
worse on the hardware Raye cares about: it asks a hard disk, whose
seeks take milliseconds, to meet a target set for flash, and the way
wbt meets a target it cannot reach is by holding writes back. A gain on
an SSD and a loss on a hard disk would cancel out in a batch
measurement and neither would be seen.

From XanMod's 7.0 series.

--- a/block/blk-wbt.c
+++ b/block/blk-wbt.c
@@ -797,12 +797,7 @@
 
 static u64 wbt_default_latency_nsec(struct request_queue *q)
 {
-	/*
-	 * We default to 2msec for non-rotational storage, and 75msec
-	 * for rotational storage.
-	 */
-	if (blk_queue_rot(q))
-		return 75000000ULL;
+	/* XanMod defaults to 2msec for any type of storage */
 	return 2000000ULL;
 }
